---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { BOOKING_URL } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="Who I work with | My Myo"
			description="James Maddock's clinical experience with hypermobility and hEDS, perimenopause and menopause, neurodivergent clients, oncology-adjacent support, and trans-affirming myotherapy in Carlton North, Melbourne."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>Who I work with</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				After nearly two decades in practice, certain people keep finding their way
				to my table. These are the populations I've spent the most time with, and
				where I can honestly say the extra experience counts.
			</p>

			<h2 id="hypermobility">Hypermobility and hEDS</h2>
			<p>
				Hypermobile joints move further than typical range. Sometimes that's harmless
				(and, in the right sport, an advantage). Sometimes it comes with pain,
				instability, and a nervous system that has spent years bracing against a body
				it doesn't quite trust. If your joints have always been "double-jointed", and
				especially if you have (or suspect) hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, the
				important thing to know is this: your pain is often coming from muscles
				working overtime to hold you together, not from the bendy joints themselves.
			</p>
			<p>
				That changes how treatment works. Stretching hypermobile tissue further is
				rarely the answer; building confident, well-coordinated strength within your
				safe range usually is. I assess before treating, work within your range,
				and pace everything to your nervous system. (If a flare-up follows a session,
				that's information too — we adjust, not push through.)
			</p>

			<h2 id="perimenopause-menopause">Perimenopause and menopause</h2>
			<p>
				Roughly half my caseload is women over 40, and what they tell me most often
				isn't about their backs. It's that nobody connected their changing bodies to
				their pain. Falling oestrogen affects collagen, muscle recovery, joint
				support, and how sensitive your nervous system is — so pain that "came out of
				nowhere" at 47 very often didn't.
			</p>
			<p>
				I can't treat menopause, and I won't pretend to. What I can do is take your
				presentation seriously, explain what's mechanically going on, keep you moving
				confidently through the transition, and work with your GP where hormonal or
				medical input is needed.
			</p>

			<h2 id="neurodivergent">Neurodivergent clients</h2>
			<p>
				My treatments are sensory-heavy experiences: touch, pressure, music, room,
				questions. So I make all of it adjustable. Pressure, pace, lighting, how
				much we talk (or don't), whether you want a running commentary on what I'm
				doing and why — it's your session, and you get to decide. That's true for
				everyone, but I know it can be the difference between an unbearable hour and
				a genuinely regulating one for autistic and ADHD clients.
			</p>
			<p>
				Practically: wear whatever is comfortable, skip the small talk if you'd
				rather, tell me at any point if something needs to change, and bring any
				sensory preferences along. I'd rather adjust the room ten times than have you
				endure it once.
			</p>

			<h2 id="oncology">Oncology-adjacent support</h2>
			<p>
				I don't treat cancer; I support the people living with and beyond it. In
				practice that's mostly gentle, well-cleared work: manual lymphatic drainage
				for post-treatment lymphoedema and surgical recovery (I've practised MLD
				continuously since 2007), scar and tissue work where appropriate, and pain
				and fatigue management at a pace your body sets.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you're in active treatment, I work with your oncology or surgical team's
				clearance, not around them. And if what you need is a credentialled
				lymphoedema therapist or a compression garment fitter, I'll point you to
				exactly the right person and be glad I did.
			</p>

			<h2 id="trans">Trans and gender-diverse clients</h2>
			<p>
				You shouldn't have to educate your therapist. My practice is gender-affirming
				as a baseline: your name and pronouns, no interrogation about your body, and
				treatment that works with the body you have now — including post-surgical
				tissue and scar work where that's what you're coming for.
			</p>
			<p>
				As with everyone: you set the comfort level on clothing and draping, we
				talk about what's being treated and why before anything happens, and nothing
				proceeds without your consent. The treatment table is one place you should
				get to just be a person with a sore shoulder.
			</p>

			<p>
				<a class="cta-button" href={BOOKING_URL} target="_blank">Book an appointment</a>
			</p>
			<p>
				See <a href="/services">the full toolkit</a> or read
				<a href="/what-is-myotherapy">what Clinical Myotherapy actually is</a>. Not sure
				you fit a box above? <a href="/contact">Send me a note</a> — if I'm not the right
				practitioner for you, I'll tell you and refer you on.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
